<!-- ========== OVERVIEW ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Historical chronology organizes the past according to time. It provides a framework for
    understanding sequence, continuity, change, turning points, and the relationships between
    developments occurring in different societies and regions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Malone Global University divides general world history into six broad chronological periods:
    Prehistory, Ancient History, Post-Classical and Medieval History, Early Modern History,
    Modern History, and Contemporary History. These divisions provide a consistent organizational
    structure while allowing individual regions and subjects to use more precise local or
    specialized periodizations when necessary.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ========== PERIODIZATION ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Understanding Historical Periodization</h2>
  <p>
    Historical periods are analytical tools rather than absolute boundaries. Political systems,
    technologies, cultures, economies, and institutions did not change everywhere at the same
    moment. A date used to mark the beginning or end of a period therefore represents a useful
    point of reference rather than a universal historical transition.
  </p>

  <p>
    Regional histories may use chronologies better suited to their own historical development.
    European history, for example, may use the Early, High, and Late Middle Ages, while Chinese
    history may be organized according to dynasties and African histories may use political,
    archaeological, environmental, or regional frameworks. These systems can coexist within the
    broader chronological structure used by the History Department.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ========== PREHISTORY ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Prehistory</h2>
  <p><strong>Before c. 3500 BCE</strong></p>

  <p>
    Prehistory examines the human past before the widespread use of written records. It includes
    human evolution, migration, hunter-gatherer societies, the development of tools and symbolic
    culture, the emergence of agriculture, permanent settlements, and the social transformations
    that preceded the first written civilizations.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because writing developed at different times in different parts of the world, the end of
    prehistory varies by region.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="prehistory/index">Explore Prehistory</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ========== ANCIENT HISTORY ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Ancient History</h2>
  <p><strong>c. 3500 BCE–500 CE</strong></p>

  <p>
    Ancient History begins with the emergence of written records, cities, organized states, and
    complex political institutions in several regions of the world. It includes the development
    of early civilizations, long-distance trade, organized religion, law, imperial systems,
    philosophy, science, warfare, and the political traditions of the ancient world.
  </p>

  <p>
    This period includes societies such as Mesopotamia, Ancient Egypt, Persia, Greece, Rome,
    early India, early imperial China, and numerous African, American, and Asian civilizations.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="ancient-history/index">Explore Ancient History</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ========== POST-CLASSICAL AND MEDIEVAL HISTORY ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Post-Classical and Medieval History</h2>
  <p><strong>c. 500–1500</strong></p>

  <p>
    Post-Classical and Medieval History examines the millennium following the transformation of
    many ancient political systems. The period saw the emergence and expansion of new states,
    religions, trade networks, military systems, intellectual traditions, and regional
    civilizations across Africa, Asia, Europe, the Middle East, and the Americas.
  </p>

  <p>
    The term <em>Middle Ages</em> remains useful for European history, while
    <em>Post-Classical</em> provides a broader framework for studying developments throughout
    the world during the same general period.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="post-classical-and-medieval-history/index">Explore Post-Classical and Medieval History</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ========== EARLY MODERN HISTORY ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Early Modern History</h2>
  <p><strong>c. 1500–1800</strong></p>

  <p>
    Early Modern History examines a period of expanding global interaction, maritime exploration,
    imperial expansion, religious transformation, commercial growth, scientific development,
    and increasingly interconnected regional economies.
  </p>

  <p>
    Major developments include European overseas expansion, the growth of the Ottoman, Safavid,
    Mughal, Ming, and Qing states, the Atlantic slave trade, colonization of the Americas,
    religious reformations, the Scientific Revolution, the Enlightenment, and the beginnings of
    industrial transformation.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="early-modern-history/index">Explore Early Modern History</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ========== MODERN HISTORY ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Modern History</h2>
  <p><strong>c. 1800–1945</strong></p>

  <p>
    Modern History examines the rapid political, economic, technological, and social changes
    associated with industrialization, nationalism, imperialism, mass politics, global migration,
    capitalism, modern state formation, and industrial warfare.
  </p>

  <p>
    The period includes the revolutionary and industrial age, nineteenth-century state and
    empire building, the expansion of colonial rule, new political ideologies, World War I,
    the interwar period, and World War II.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="modern-history/index">Explore Modern History</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ========== CONTEMPORARY HISTORY ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Contemporary History</h2>
  <p><strong>1945–Present</strong></p>

  <p>
    Contemporary History begins with the end of World War II and examines the political,
    economic, social, cultural, and technological developments that have shaped the present
    international system.
  </p>

  <p>
    Major themes include the Cold War, nuclear weapons, decolonization, postcolonial state
    formation, civil rights movements, globalization, international institutions, economic
    integration, digital technology, changing forms of warfare, and the political and social
    developments of the twenty-first century.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="contemporary-history/index">Explore Contemporary History</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ========== MASTER CHRONOLOGY ========== -->
<section>
  <h2>Master Chronological Framework</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prehistory:</strong> before c. 3500 BCE</li>
    <li><strong>Ancient History:</strong> c. 3500 BCE–500 CE</li>
    <li><strong>Post-Classical and Medieval History:</strong> c. 500–1500</li>
    <li><strong>Early Modern History:</strong> c. 1500–1800</li>
    <li><strong>Modern History:</strong> c. 1800–1945</li>
    <li><strong>Contemporary History:</strong> 1945–Present</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ========== HISTORICAL CONNECTIONS ========== -->
<section>
